        "type": "reasoning_text",
        "text": "Important governance facts found:\n1. 控股股东 = 康哲药业 (CMS, 00867.HK) — 大股东康哲药业及其一致行动人 (西藏康哲企业管理、深圳市康哲药业、天津康哲维盛医药科技)\n2. Critical governance risk: 关联交易 — 新活素、依姆多、雪山金罗汉止痛涂膜剂交给大股东康哲做推广（独家推广服务），推广费巨大：\"超四成营收流向大股东\" (huanqiu article). 2026年预计与康哲药业关联交易推广费不超17.8亿元 — that's on 33亿 revenue budget, meaning ~50%+ of revenue goes to the parent as promotion fees! This is a MAJOR governance risk from 李录's perspective — the \"富了方丈穷了庙\" (rich monks, poor temple) criticism.\n3. 2019 推广费 5.29亿; now 2026 预计 17.8亿.\n\nThis is exactly the kind of risk the 李录 risk assessment should highlight — related-party transactions with controlling shareholder, profit leakage. But wait — I need to be careful about the direction of this: does the promotion fee make the listed company's profit understate or overstate? The concern: 康哲 (the parent) takes the marketing/promotion function, earning the promotion fees. The listed company 西藏药业 does production and collects revenue at ex-factory price? Actually no — for 新活素, 西藏药业 holds the批文 and sells the product; 康哲 does CSO promotion. The promotion fee is booked as 销售费用 by 西藏药业 and paid to 康哲 subsidiaries. So revenue flows through 西藏药业's P&L but a huge chunk of it goes to the parent as sales expense. \"超四成营收流向大股东\" — over 40% of revenue flows to the controlling shareholder. That's a related-party transaction governance concern: pricing of promotion services, transfer of value to parent.\n\nLet me get a bit more detail on this. Let me read the huanqiu article briefly. Also check for 质押/减持. Let me fetch the article."
      }
